(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[25] = {"n":25,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 25% · B2 50% · B2+ 25%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Gene editing to improve crops","p":["For thousands of years, farmers have improved crops by selecting and breeding plants with useful characteristics, such as larger grains, sweeter fruit or resistance to disease. This traditional breeding has been enormously successful, but it is slow. Because desirable traits must be combined through many generations of crossing and selection, developing a new variety can take ten years or more. Today, a technology known as gene editing promises to make this process much faster and more precise.","The most widely used gene-editing tool is called CRISPR-Cas9. It was adapted from a natural defence system that bacteria use to fight viruses, and its development earned the scientists Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2020. CRISPR works like a pair of molecular scissors guided by a kind of address label. Scientists design a short piece of genetic material that matches a specific location in a plant's DNA. The system finds this location and cuts the DNA there, allowing researchers to switch off a gene, alter it slightly or, in some cases, insert new genetic material.","It is important to distinguish gene editing from the older technology of genetic modification, which produced so-called GM crops. Traditional genetic modification usually involves inserting a gene from a different species, for example a gene from a bacterium that helps a plant resist insects. Many forms of gene editing, by contrast, simply make small changes to a plant's own genes, similar to the mutations that occur naturally or through conventional breeding. For this reason, some countries regulate gene-edited crops less strictly than GM crops, while others treat them in the same way.","Researchers have already used gene editing to produce a wide range of improved crops. Scientists have developed wheat that resists a common fungal disease, tomatoes that contain higher levels of healthy compounds, and rice varieties that tolerate flooding or produce more grain. Gene editing could also help crops cope with the challenges of climate change, such as heat, drought and salty soil. This is of particular interest in arid countries, including Saudi Arabia, where researchers at King Abdullah University of Science and Technology have studied ways to improve the tolerance of plants to heat and salinity.","However, the technology also raises concerns. Some critics worry about unintended effects if the editing tool cuts DNA in the wrong place, although scientists say that such errors can be detected and that improved methods are making them rarer. Others are concerned about the concentration of power in a few large companies that may hold patents on gene-edited seeds, potentially making farmers dependent on them. There are also questions about labelling: should consumers be told that a food has been gene-edited, even if the change could have occurred naturally?","Supporters argue that gene editing offers a valuable tool for feeding a growing world population while reducing the use of pesticides and water. Most experts agree, however, that technology alone cannot solve the problem of hunger, which is also linked to poverty, conflict and food waste. As with many powerful technologies, the benefits of gene editing will depend on how wisely it is regulated and how fairly its benefits are shared."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of traditional farming","how gene editing can improve crops and the debates it raises","how bacteria fight viruses","the life of two Nobel Prize winners"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains the technology and concerns.","h":"تحرير الجينات في المحاصيل."},{"n":2,"s":"What is the main disadvantage of traditional breeding mentioned in the passage?","o":["It is very dangerous.","It has never been successful.","It requires expensive machines.","It takes a long time."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Developing a variety can take ten years.","h":"بطء العملية."},{"n":3,"s":"Why does the writer compare CRISPR to 'molecular scissors guided by an address label'?","o":["to show that it is used in offices","to suggest that it is dangerous","to explain how it finds and cuts a specific part of DNA","to describe how plants grow"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"The analogy explains its function.","h":"تشبيه لتوضيح العمل."},{"n":4,"s":"How do many forms of gene editing differ from traditional genetic modification?","o":["They always insert genes from animals.","They do not change DNA at all.","They are illegal in all countries.","They make small changes to a plant's own genes."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains the difference.","h":"تعديلات صغيرة على جينات النبات."},{"n":5,"s":"In paragraph 3, the word 'them' in 'treat them in the same way' refers to","o":["gene-edited crops","bacteria","insects","some countries"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"Some countries treat gene-edited crops like GM crops.","h":"تعود على المحاصيل المعدلة."},{"n":6,"s":"The word 'tolerate' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["cause","avoid completely","measure","survive in difficult conditions"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Rice that can survive flooding.","h":"يتحمل."},{"n":7,"s":"Why does the writer mention King Abdullah University of Science and Technology?","o":["to criticize its farming methods","as an example of research on crops for hot, dry conditions","to show where CRISPR was invented","to compare it with American universities"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Its research on heat and salinity.","h":"مثال على أبحاث محلية."},{"n":8,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a concern about gene editing?","o":["The tool may cut DNA in the wrong place.","Gene-edited crops taste worse.","A few companies may control seeds.","Consumers may not be informed through labels."],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Taste is not mentioned.","h":"الطعم غير مذكور."},{"n":9,"s":"What can be inferred about the writer's view of hunger?","o":["It can be completely solved by gene editing.","It is caused only by climate change.","It is no longer a serious problem.","It has many causes, so technology alone is not enough."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Hunger is linked to poverty, conflict and waste.","h":"أسباب متعددة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's overall attitude towards gene editing?","o":["strongly opposed","completely uncritical","cautiously positive","indifferent"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"Benefits are presented with conditions.","h":"إيجابي بحذر."}]},{"t":"Dilmun: an ancient Gulf civilization","p":["When people think of the great civilizations of the ancient Middle East, they usually think of Mesopotamia and Egypt. Yet in the third and second millennia BCE, a prosperous trading civilization flourished along the western shores of the Arabian Gulf. Known from ancient texts as Dilmun, it was centred on the islands of Bahrain and extended, at various times, to the eastern coast of what is now Saudi Arabia, including Tarout Island and the oasis of Al-Ahsa, as well as Failaka Island in Kuwait.","Dilmun appears frequently in the writings of ancient Mesopotamia, the land between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers. Sumerian texts describe it as a pure and holy land where there was no sickness or death, and some myths present it as a kind of paradise. Other documents are more practical: they record shipments of goods passing through Dilmun's ports. For a long time, however, scholars were uncertain where Dilmun had actually been located. Some suggested places as far away as the Indus Valley or southern Iran, while others believed it might be purely mythical.","The answer came from archaeology. In the 1950s, a Danish expedition led by Geoffrey Bibby and Peter Glob began excavating in Bahrain. They uncovered the remains of a large settlement, now known as Qal'at al-Bahrain, along with temples and thousands of distinctive circular stone seals. Inscriptions and the style of the finds confirmed that Bahrain had been the heart of ancient Dilmun. Excavations in eastern Saudi Arabia have since revealed further evidence of the civilization, including a remarkable statue and other objects from Tarout.","Dilmun's wealth came largely from its position. It lay on the sea route between Mesopotamia and two other important regions: Magan, generally identified with present-day Oman, which supplied copper, and Meluhha, the Indus Valley civilization in what is now Pakistan and India, which supplied timber, precious stones and ivory. Dilmun's merchants acted as middlemen, transporting goods between these regions and taking a share of the profits. Standard weights based on the Indus system were used in Dilmun, which suggests close commercial connections.","Fresh water was another key to Dilmun's success. Although the region is extremely dry, underground springs on the islands and along the coast provided water for drinking and for date palm gardens. Some springs even bubbled up through the seabed, allowing sailors to collect fresh water from the sea itself. This unusual feature may help to explain why Mesopotamian texts associated Dilmun with purity and abundance.","One of the most striking features of the Dilmun landscape is its burial mounds. Tens of thousands of these mounds once covered large areas of Bahrain, making it one of the largest ancient cemeteries in the world. Some were small, while others, apparently built for rulers, were large and elaborate. In 2019, the burial mounds were added to UNESCO's World Heritage List. Dilmun's influence declined after about 1700 BCE, possibly because of changes in trade routes and the decline of the Indus civilization. Nevertheless, its story shows that the Gulf region played a central role in the networks that connected the ancient world."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Rivers of Mesopotamia","How to Excavate a Temple","Dilmun: A Trading Centre of the Ancient Gulf","Modern Trade in the Arabian Gulf"],"k":2,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage describes Dilmun.","h":"دلمون مركز تجاري."},{"n":12,"s":"According to the passage, where was the centre of Dilmun?","o":["the islands of Bahrain","the Indus Valley","central Mesopotamia","southern Oman"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 states this.","h":"البحرين."},{"n":13,"s":"How do Sumerian myths describe Dilmun?","o":["as a dangerous place full of enemies","as a pure land without sickness or death","as a cold and empty desert","as a city of soldiers"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 describes this.","h":"أرض طاهرة."},{"n":14,"s":"What confirmed that Bahrain had been the heart of Dilmun?","o":["descriptions in Greek books","satellite photographs","inscriptions and the style of archaeological finds","the memories of local people"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"النقوش وأسلوب المكتشفات."},{"n":15,"s":"The word 'middlemen' in paragraph 4 refers to people who","o":["produce copper","build ships","transport and trade goods between producers and buyers","rule cities"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Dilmun's merchants connected regions.","h":"الوسطاء."},{"n":16,"s":"What does the use of Indus-style weights in Dilmun suggest?","o":["Dilmun had close trade links with the Indus Valley.","Dilmun was ruled by the Indus Valley.","Dilmun had no system of weights of its own before trade.","Dilmun did not trade with Mesopotamia."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"It 'suggests close commercial connections'.","h":"روابط تجارية وثيقة."},{"n":17,"s":"Why does the writer mention springs that bubbled up through the seabed?","o":["to show that the sea was dangerous","to describe how pearls were collected","to help explain why Dilmun was linked with purity and abundance","to explain why trade declined"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"It may explain the Mesopotamian descriptions.","h":"تفسير الصورة المثالية."},{"n":18,"s":"In paragraph 6, the word 'its' in 'its story shows' refers to","o":["UNESCO","Dilmun","the Indus civilization","the Gulf region"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"Dilmun's story.","h":"تعود على دلمون."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following happened LAST?","o":["The burial mounds were added to the World Heritage List.","A Danish expedition began excavating in Bahrain.","Dilmun's influence began to decline.","Dilmun traded with Magan and Meluhha."],"k":0,"ty":"sequence","e":"2019 is the latest event.","h":"٢٠١٩ هو الأخير."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Dilmun was more powerful than Egypt.","Dilmun was never a real place.","Ancient trade was unimportant.","The Gulf region was central to ancient trade networks."],"k":3,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final sentence.","h":"دور الخليج المحوري."}]},{"t":"The psychology of queues","p":["Waiting in line is an unavoidable part of modern life. We queue at supermarkets, banks, airports and government offices, and studies suggest that people in large cities may spend weeks of their lives standing in lines. Although queues are often treated as a purely practical problem, to be solved by adding more staff or faster systems, researchers have discovered that the experience of waiting depends at least as much on psychology as on the actual time involved. Two people who wait for exactly the same length of time may describe the experience in completely different ways.","One of the most famous examples comes from an airport in Houston, in the United States. Passengers complained repeatedly about long waits at the baggage claim. The airport hired more staff, reducing the average wait to eight minutes, but complaints continued. Investigators then noticed that passengers walked only about one minute from their plane to the baggage area and then stood waiting for seven minutes. The airport's solution was not to make bags arrive faster, but to move arrival gates further away, so that passengers walked for six minutes and waited for only two. Complaints almost disappeared.","This case illustrates a central principle: occupied time feels shorter than unoccupied time. For this reason, many businesses try to keep waiting customers busy. Mirrors are placed beside lifts, screens show news or advertisements, and restaurants give customers menus to read while they wait for a table. Similarly, uncertain waits feel longer than known, finite waits. Displays that show the expected waiting time, or the number of people ahead, can make waiting more bearable, even if the actual time does not change.","Fairness is another key factor. People become frustrated when they feel that others are being served out of turn. Research has shown that customers generally prefer a single long line feeding several service points to separate lines for each counter, even though the average waiting time is similar. With separate lines, people constantly compare their progress with others and feel annoyed if a neighbouring line moves faster. A single line guarantees that customers are served in order of arrival.","The way a wait ends also matters. Psychologists have found that people's memory of an experience is strongly influenced by how it finishes. If a long wait ends with friendly, efficient service, customers often remember the whole experience more positively. For this reason, some theme parks deliberately overestimate waiting times, so that visitors are pleasantly surprised when they reach the front sooner than expected.","Cultural expectations also affect behaviour in queues. In some societies, forming orderly lines is a strong social norm, while in others, waiting is organized differently, for example by taking numbered tickets. Technology is increasingly changing the experience: many banks, hospitals and government service centres in Saudi Arabia now allow people to book appointments or join virtual queues through mobile apps, receiving a notification when it is their turn. Such systems may not eliminate waiting, but by giving people more control over their time, they can make it far less frustrating."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how psychology affects the experience of waiting","how airports handle luggage","the history of supermarkets","why banks need more staff"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage discusses the psychology of queues.","h":"سيكولوجية الانتظار."},{"n":22,"s":"Why did complaints continue after the Houston airport hired more staff?","o":["The bags took longer to arrive.","Passengers still spent most of the time standing and waiting.","The staff were unfriendly.","The airport was too crowded."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"They walked one minute and waited seven.","h":"معظم الوقت انتظار."},{"n":23,"s":"What was the airport's solution?","o":["hiring even more staff","reducing the number of flights","giving passengers free food","moving arrival gates further away from the baggage area"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"إبعاد البوابات."},{"n":24,"s":"Why are mirrors placed beside lifts?","o":["to make the area look bigger","to improve security","to save electricity","to keep waiting people occupied"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Occupied time feels shorter.","h":"إشغال المنتظرين."},{"n":25,"s":"The word 'bearable' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["more expensive","easier to accept","faster","more confusing"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Displays make waiting easier to tolerate.","h":"محتمل."},{"n":26,"s":"Why do many people prefer a single line feeding several counters?","o":["It is always much faster.","It requires fewer staff.","It allows people to choose their counter.","It ensures customers are served in order of arrival."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"العدالة في الترتيب."},{"n":27,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'others' in 'compare their progress with others' refers to","o":["people in other lines","service counters","researchers","staff members"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"People compare themselves with those in other lines.","h":"تعود على الآخرين في الطوابير."},{"n":28,"s":"What can be inferred about theme parks that overestimate waiting times?","o":["They want visitors to feel pleasantly surprised.","They want visitors to leave the line.","They do not know how long the waits are.","They are trying to reduce ticket prices."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Visitors reach the front sooner than expected.","h":"مفاجأة سارة."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way of making waiting less frustrating?","o":["showing the expected waiting time","keeping people busy","charging customers for faster service","using virtual queues through apps"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Paid faster service is not mentioned.","h":"الدفع غير مذكور."},{"n":30,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It presents several psychological principles with examples.","It tells the story of one customer.","It compares two countries' laws.","It argues that queues should be banned."],"k":0,"ty":"organization","e":"Each paragraph presents a principle.","h":"مبادئ مع أمثلة."}]},{"t":"Ocean acidification","p":["Most discussions of carbon dioxide focus on its role in warming the atmosphere. However, a large proportion of the carbon dioxide released by human activities does not stay in the air. Since the start of the Industrial Revolution, the oceans have absorbed roughly a quarter of it. This has slowed global warming, but it has come at a cost. When carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, it triggers chemical reactions that make the water more acidic, a process known as ocean acidification.","Scientists measure acidity using the pH scale, on which lower numbers indicate greater acidity. The average pH of surface ocean water has fallen from about 8.2 before the industrial era to about 8.1 today. This may appear to be a tiny change, but the pH scale is logarithmic, which means that a decrease of 0.1 represents an increase in acidity of about thirty per cent. If emissions continue at high levels, the pH could fall further by the end of this century, to levels the oceans have not experienced for millions of years.","The main concern is the effect on marine organisms that build shells or skeletons from calcium carbonate. These include corals, oysters, mussels, sea urchins and many types of plankton. As seawater becomes more acidic, the concentration of carbonate ions, which these organisms need, decreases. Building shells therefore requires more energy, and in extreme cases, existing shells may begin to dissolve. Experiments have shown that the larvae of some shellfish grow more slowly and develop deformed shells in more acidic water.","Coral reefs are especially vulnerable. Reefs are built over centuries by tiny animals that produce calcium carbonate skeletons, and they support about a quarter of all marine species, although they cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor. Already threatened by rising temperatures, which cause corals to lose the algae that provide their colour and much of their food, reefs may find it harder to grow and recover when the water is also more acidic. The Red Sea contains some of the world's most spectacular coral reefs, and scientists are studying them closely, as some of its corals appear unusually tolerant of high temperatures.","The effects could spread throughout marine food webs. Tiny shell-forming plankton are eaten by fish, which are in turn eaten by larger animals, including humans. Acidification may also affect fish behaviour; some studies have suggested that young fish in acidic water become less able to detect predators, although later research has questioned how strong this effect is. For communities that depend on fishing and shellfish farming, the economic consequences could be serious.","Unlike some environmental problems, ocean acidification cannot be solved locally. The only lasting solution is to reduce global emissions of carbon dioxide. However, local actions can help ecosystems cope, such as reducing pollution, protecting marine areas from overfishing and restoring coastal habitats like seagrass meadows and mangroves, which absorb carbon dioxide. Ocean acidification is sometimes called 'the other carbon dioxide problem', a reminder that the consequences of our emissions reach far beyond the air we breathe."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the causes and effects of the ocean becoming more acidic","how to measure air pollution","the history of the Industrial Revolution","how fish are farmed"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains ocean acidification.","h":"تحمض المحيطات."},{"n":32,"s":"What happens when carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater?","o":["The water becomes warmer immediately.","Chemical reactions make the water more acidic.","The water becomes less salty.","Shells grow more quickly."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"تزداد الحموضة."},{"n":33,"s":"Why does the writer explain that the pH scale is logarithmic?","o":["to show that the ocean is not changing","to compare the ocean with rivers","to explain how the scale was invented","to show that a small change in pH is significant"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"A fall of 0.1 means about 30% more acidity.","h":"تغير صغير مهم."},{"n":34,"s":"Why do shell-building organisms find it harder to survive in more acidic water?","o":["There are fewer carbonate ions available to build shells.","The water is too cold.","There is too much food.","Predators can see them more easily."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"نقص أيونات الكربونات."},{"n":35,"s":"The word 'deformed' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["very strong","brightly coloured","badly shaped","unusually large"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Shells that do not develop properly.","h":"مشوه."},{"n":36,"s":"According to the passage, how much of the ocean floor do coral reefs cover?","o":["about a quarter","about half","less than one per cent","more than ten per cent"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"أقل من واحد بالمئة."},{"n":37,"s":"What can be inferred about the Red Sea's corals?","o":["They have already died.","They are not affected by acidification.","They are the smallest in the world.","They may help scientists understand how corals cope with heat."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Some appear unusually tolerant of heat.","h":"قد تساعد على فهم التحمل."},{"n":38,"s":"What does the writer suggest about research on fish behaviour?","o":["It proves that all fish will die.","Its findings are not fully certain.","It has never been questioned.","It shows that fish prefer acidic water."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Later research questioned the effect.","h":"نتائج غير مؤكدة."},{"n":39,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a local action that can help?","o":["reducing pollution","protecting areas from overfishing","adding chemicals to make the sea less acidic","restoring seagrass meadows and mangroves"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Adding chemicals is not mentioned.","h":"إضافة المواد الكيميائية غير مذكورة."},{"n":40,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Oceans are absorbing less carbon dioxide than before.","Carbon dioxide is making oceans more acidic, threatening marine life, and global emissions must be reduced.","Ocean acidification can be solved by local fishing rules.","Coral reefs are not affected by carbon dioxide."],"k":1,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main points.","h":"ملخص شامل."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Only when the lights went out ______ how late it was.","o":["they noticed","noticed they","did they notice","they did notice"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did they notice — Only when … at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Only when."},{"n":42,"s":"If he were more organized, he ______ his deadline last week.","o":["wouldn't have missed","wouldn't miss","won't miss","hadn't missed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't have missed — Mixed conditional: present state, past result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ from the top of the tower, the city looks completely different.","o":["Seeing","Seen","To see","Having seeing"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Seen — Past participle clause.","h":"اسم المفعول."},{"n":44,"s":"The committee proposed that the meeting ______ until next month.","o":["is postponing","postpones","will postponed","be postponed"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"be postponed — Passive subjunctive.","h":"صيغة الطلب للمجهول."},{"n":45,"s":"The company is reported ______ more than five hundred new employees this year.","o":["having hired","to hiring","to have hired","that hired"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have hired — Passive reporting: to have + p.p.","h":"is reported to have."},{"n":46,"s":"My brother wishes he ______ medicine instead of law at university.","o":["studied","had studied","would study","has studied"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had studied — wish + past perfect.","h":"wish مع الماضي التام."},{"n":47,"s":"All I want ______ a few hours of peace and quiet.","o":["are","is","be","being"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is — Cleft with All: singular verb.","h":"All … is."},{"n":48,"s":"At no time ______ that the project would fail.","o":["did the manager suggest","the manager suggested","suggested the manager","the manager did suggest"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did the manager suggest — At no time: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد At no time."},{"n":49,"s":"She has three brothers, none of ______ live in Riyadh.","o":["who","whom","them","which"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whom — none of whom.","h":"none of whom."},{"n":50,"s":"______ the weather be bad, the ceremony will take place indoors.","o":["Would","Had","If","Should"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Should — Should at the start = if.","h":"Should بدل If."},{"n":51,"s":"When I finally called him, he ______ for my message all afternoon.","o":["has been waiting","was waited","had been waiting","waits"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been waiting — Past perfect continuous.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"The athlete admitted ______ the rules of the competition.","o":["to break","break","having broken","to have breaking"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"having broken — admit + -ing.","h":"admit + -ing."},{"n":53,"s":"The students succeeded ______ a working robot in just two weeks.","o":["to build","in building","for building","on build"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"in building — succeed in + -ing.","h":"succeed in."},{"n":54,"s":"Take some snacks ______ you get hungry during the journey.","o":["in case","unless","so that not","provided"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"in case — in case = as a precaution.","h":"in case."},{"n":55,"s":"It's about time we ______ a decision about the new office.","o":["make","will make","have made","made"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"made — It's about time + past.","h":"It's about time."},{"n":56,"s":"The results ______ until all the data have been checked.","o":["will not be announced","will not announce","are not announcing","have not announced"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will not be announced — Future passive.","h":"المستقبل المبني للمجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ being the youngest player, he scored the winning goal.","o":["In spite of","Although","Even","However"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"In spite of — In spite of + -ing.","h":"In spite of."},{"n":58,"s":"The guide warned the tourists ______ too close to the edge.","o":["not going","to not going","don't go","not to go"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"not to go — warn + object + not to.","h":"warn sb not to."},{"n":59,"s":"The longer you wait, ______ it will be to find a seat.","o":["harder","the hardest","the harder","more hard"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the harder — Double comparative.","h":"the … the."},{"n":60,"s":"I would just as soon ______ the bus as drive in this traffic.","o":["taking","to take","took","take"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"take — would (just) as soon + base verb.","h":"would as soon."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"(A) Much students (B) have (C) asked for (D) extra lessons.","o":["Much","have","asked","extra"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Much — students is countable: Many.","h":"Many مع المعدود."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) spoke (B) so (C) quiet that nobody (D) heard her.","o":["spoke","so","quiet","heard"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"quiet — An adverb is needed: quietly.","h":"الظرف quietly."},{"n":63,"s":"He (A) has (B) went to the bank (C) to (D) pay the bill.","o":["has","went","to","pay"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"went — Present perfect: has gone.","h":"التصريف الثالث gone."},{"n":64,"s":"I'm not used (A) to (B) driving on the (C) left, so I drive very (D) slow.","o":["to","driving","left","slow"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"slow — An adverb is needed: slowly.","h":"الظرف slowly."},{"n":65,"s":"The scientists (A) who (B) discovers the new species (C) were (D) interviewed on television.","o":["who","discovers","were","interviewed"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"discovers — Past tense and plural subject: discovered.","h":"discovered."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) In spite the delay, the passengers (B) remained (C) calm and (D) patient.","o":["In spite","remained","calm","patient"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"In spite — In spite of + noun.","h":"In spite of."},{"n":67,"s":"The children (A) enjoyed (B) themselves at the party and (C) did not want to (D) leaving.","o":["enjoyed","themselves","did not","leaving"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"leaving — want to + base verb: leave.","h":"to + المصدر."},{"n":68,"s":"Had I (A) known about the meeting, I (B) would (C) attended it (D) on time.","o":["known","would","attended","on"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"attended — Third conditional: would have attended.","h":"would have attended."},{"n":69,"s":"(A) Whom do you think (B) will (C) win the (D) competition?","o":["Whom","will","win","competition"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Whom — Subject of 'will win': Who.","h":"Who فاعل."},{"n":70,"s":"This is the (A) most (B) interested book I have (C) ever (D) read.","o":["most","interested","ever","read"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"interested — -ing adjective: interesting.","h":"interesting."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M25-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What is the woman suggesting?","o":["The report is already finished.","She does not want to write the report.","The deadline is not realistic.","The sales data is wrong."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"She explains why Thursday is too soon.","h":"الموعد غير واقعي."}],"t":"A hint that a deadline is unrealistic","sc":["Situation: A manager and an employee are talking. (62 words)","Man: I'd like the full market report finished by Thursday. Is that possible?","Woman: Well, we haven't received the sales data from the regional offices yet, and the analysis usually takes at least a week once we have it.","Man: So Thursday is a bit ambitious?","Woman: I could give you a summary of the early findings by Thursday, and the complete report the following Wednesday."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M25-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"How does the second man feel about the diet?","o":["He wants to try it himself.","He doubts that it is healthy.","He thinks it is a brilliant idea.","He is not interested at all."],"k":1,"ty":"opinion","e":"He questions it and suggests a nutritionist.","h":"يشك في فائدته."}],"t":"A friend's doubt about a diet plan","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (61 words)","Man: I've started a new diet. I'm only eating fruit for the next month.","Man: Only fruit? For a whole month? Are you sure that's a good idea?","Man: A website said it's very healthy.","Man: I'm not a doctor, but you'd be missing protein and lots of other things. Why not speak to a nutritionist first? They could design a plan that's actually balanced."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M25-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What will the waiter bring while the customer waits?","o":["the bill","a different main course","a dessert","a free salad"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'a complimentary salad'.","h":"سلطة مجانية."}],"t":"A polite complaint to a waiter","sc":["Situation: A customer speaks to a waiter in a restaurant. (69 words)","Woman: Excuse me. I'm sorry to bother you, but I ordered the grilled fish about forty minutes ago, and it still hasn't arrived.","Man: I do apologize. The kitchen has been very busy tonight. Let me check on it for you.","Woman: Thank you. Also, I asked for no sauce, if that's still possible.","Man: Of course. I'll make sure the chef knows, and I'll bring you a complimentary salad while you wait."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M25-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What is the man trying to do?","o":["reject the job offer","ask for more holidays","negotiate a higher salary","change departments"],"k":2,"ty":"function","e":"He asks about flexibility in salary.","h":"التفاوض على الراتب."}],"t":"An indirect question about a salary offer","sc":["Situation: A job candidate speaks to a human resources officer. (67 words)","Man: Thank you for the offer. I'm very interested in the position. I was just wondering, though, whether there's any flexibility in the starting salary.","Woman: What did you have in mind?","Man: Considering my master's degree and three years of experience, I was hoping for something closer to the upper end of the range advertised.","Woman: I'll discuss it with the department head and get back to you by Sunday."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M25-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"How does the second woman feel?","o":["She is angry with Nora.","She is glad she stayed at home.","She is uninterested in scholarships.","She regrets not applying."],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'I really should have tried.'","h":"ندم."}],"t":"Regret about a missed opportunity","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (60 words)","Woman: Did you hear that Nora got the scholarship to study in Japan?","Woman: Yes. To be honest, I'm a little jealous. I saw the advertisement too, but I didn't apply because I thought I had no chance.","Woman: Your grades are just as good as hers.","Woman: I know. I really should have tried. Next year, I'm applying for everything I'm qualified for."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M25-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Why is the man not satisfied with his review?","o":["His manager criticized him strongly.","He did not receive a review.","The feedback was too general to be useful.","He was refused a promotion."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'generally acceptable' gives no detail.","h":"ملاحظات عامة."}],"t":"A manager's vague praise","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking after a review meeting. (59 words)","Man: How did your annual review go?","Man: It was fine, I suppose. My manager said my work was 'generally acceptable' and that I should 'keep going'.","Man: That doesn't tell you much.","Man: Exactly. I'm going to ask her for specific examples of what I'm doing well and what I need to improve. Otherwise, I don't know how to get a promotion."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M25-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"Why does the woman finally accept the food?","o":["She has not cooked dinner.","Her neighbour insists and says it would be wasted.","She wants to sell it.","Her family asked her to."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'I insist. It'll only go to waste otherwise.'","h":"الإصرار وتجنب الهدر."}],"t":"A neighbour's offer that is hard to refuse","sc":["Situation: Two neighbours are talking outside their houses. (55 words)","Woman: I've made far too much kabsa for tonight. Please take some for your family.","Woman: That's so generous, but I've already cooked dinner.","Woman: Then keep it for tomorrow's lunch. I insist. It'll only go to waste otherwise.","Woman: Well, if you insist, thank you very much. I'll return your dish tomorrow, with some of my date cake."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M25-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What caused the misunderstanding?","o":["The meeting was cancelled.","The man forgot the meeting.","The internet connection failed.","The meeting time was given in a different time zone. Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"It was four o'clock London time.","h":"فارق التوقيت."}],"t":"A misunderstanding about a meeting time zone","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking about an online meeting. (58 words)","Man: Why didn't you join the online meeting with the London office? We waited for you.","Man: But the invitation said four o'clock. I joined at four, and nobody was there.","Man: Ah, it was four o'clock London time. That's six o'clock here.","Man: So I was two hours early, not late! Next time, let's make sure invitations show both time zones."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M25-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why does the second woman want to focus only on families?","o":["Restaurants refused to take part.","Families waste less food.","A broad audience would weaken the message.","Wedding halls are closed."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'If our audience is too broad, the message will be weaker.'","h":"تركيز الرسالة."},{"n":80,"s":"How will they measure the campaign's success?","o":["with surveys before and after, and website visits","by counting social media likes only","by visiting every household","by asking restaurants for figures"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"They mention both methods.","h":"استبيانات وزيارات الموقع."}],"t":"Planning a public awareness campaign","sc":["Situation: Two students are planning a campaign. (174 words)","Woman: Our assignment is to plan a public awareness campaign. I suggest we focus on reducing food waste. Saudi households throw away a surprising amount of food, especially during celebrations.","Woman: That's a good choice. Who's our target audience?","Woman: Mainly families, since most waste happens at home. But we could also target restaurants and wedding halls.","Woman: Let's keep it focused on families. If our audience is too broad, the message will be weaker.","Woman: Fair enough. What channels should we use?","Woman: Social media, definitely. Short videos showing simple tips, like planning meals, storing food properly and using leftovers creatively.","Woman: We could also partner with a food bank. They collect surplus food and distribute it to families in need. That would give people a practical action to take.","Woman: Great idea. And we need a way to measure the campaign's success. Perhaps a short survey before and after, asking people how much food they throw away.","Woman: And we could count how many people visit the food bank's website through our link.","Woman: Perfect. Let's write up the plan this weekend."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M25-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What is the problem with the student's consent form?","o":["It is too short.","It is written only in Arabic.","It does not mention the clinic.","It is too technical for participants to understand."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'Your consent form is too technical.'","h":"لغة تقنية صعبة."},{"n":82,"s":"What must participants be able to do, according to Dr. Huda?","o":["withdraw at any time without giving a reason","choose the interview questions","listen to other participants' recordings","receive payment"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated clearly.","h":"الانسحاب في أي وقت."}],"t":"Discussing a research ethics form","sc":["Situation: A student meets a member of the ethics committee. (184 words)","Man: Dr. Huda, the committee asked me to revise my ethics form. I'm not sure what I need to change.","Woman: Your study involves interviewing patients at a clinic, so the main issues are consent and privacy. First, your consent form is too technical. Participants need to understand exactly what they're agreeing to, in simple language.","Man: Should I translate it into Arabic as well?","Woman: Yes, most patients will prefer Arabic. Second, you need to explain how you'll protect their identity. Will you record the interviews?","Man: I was planning to, yes.","Woman: Then explain where the recordings will be stored, who can access them and when they'll be deleted. You should also use codes instead of names in your transcripts.","Man: And what if a participant wants to withdraw?","Woman: They must be able to withdraw at any time without giving a reason, and that must be clearly stated on the form. Make those changes and resubmit by the end of the month.","Man: Thank you. Could I send you a draft before I submit it officially?","Woman: Yes, of course. Email it to me next week, and I'll check it quickly."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M25-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why would handing out questionnaires in the library cause a problem?","o":["It would only include students who already use the library.","The library does not allow surveys.","Students would not have time to answer.","It would be too expensive."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"The results would be biased.","h":"عينة متحيزة."},{"n":84,"s":"Why do they plan to send the survey to about a thousand students?","o":["The supervisor requires a thousand answers.","Response rates for email surveys are often low.","There are only a thousand students.","Emails are free to send. Talks and lectures"],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"They need at least two hundred responses.","h":"انخفاض نسبة الاستجابة."}],"t":"Choosing a sample for a survey","sc":["Situation: Two students are discussing their research design. (184 words)","Man: Our survey is about how students use the university library. I was thinking we could just hand out questionnaires to people inside the library.","Man: But then we'd only hear from students who already use it. What about those who never go?","Man: Good point. Our results would be biased.","Man: Exactly. We need a sample that represents the whole student body. We could ask the registration office for a random list of students from all faculties.","Man: Would they give us that?","Man: Probably not names, for privacy reasons. But they might send our survey link by email to a random selection on our behalf.","Man: That would work. How many responses do we need?","Man: Our supervisor suggested at least two hundred, so we can compare faculties. Since response rates for email surveys are often low, we should send it to about a thousand students.","Man: And we should send a reminder after a week. That usually increases responses.","Man: Agreed. Let's also keep the survey short, no more than ten minutes. Long surveys put people off.","Man: I'll draft the questions tonight, and we can test them on our classmates tomorrow."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M25-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why are many desert animals nocturnal?","o":["to find mates more easily","because they cannot see in daylight","to hunt larger animals","to avoid the hottest part of the day"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"They rest during the hottest part of the day.","h":"تجنب الحر."},{"n":86,"s":"What does the lecturer say about the camel's hump?","o":["It stores fat, which helps the rest of the body lose heat.","It stores water for long journeys.","It keeps the camel warm at night.","It contains extra blood vessels."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'a camel's hump stores fat, not water'.","h":"تخزين الدهون."},{"n":87,"s":"How do camels save water during the day?","o":["by allowing their body temperature to rise instead of sweating","by drinking only at night","by staying in burrows","by eating dry seeds"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"رفع حرارة الجسم بدل التعرق."}],"t":"Extended lecture: how animals survive extreme heat","sc":["Situation: A biology lecturer speaks to students. (296 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today's lecture examines how animals survive in some of the hottest environments on Earth, including the deserts of the Arabian Peninsula, where summer temperatures can exceed fifty degrees Celsius. Animals face two related challenges in such places: keeping their bodies from overheating and conserving water. Let's begin with behaviour, which is often the first line of defence. Many desert animals are nocturnal, meaning they are active at night and rest during the hottest part of the day. Jerboas and many desert snakes, for example, spend the day in burrows, where temperatures are much lower than at the surface. Other animals move into the shade or reduce their activity at midday. Next, consider physical adaptations. The Arabian oryx has a pale, reflective coat that reduces the amount of heat it absorbs from the sun. The fennec fox and the sand cat have large ears containing many blood vessels, which help to release body heat. Camels are perhaps the most remarkable example. Contrary to popular belief, a camel's hump stores fat, not water. Concentrating fat in one place means the rest of the body has less insulation, which helps heat escape. Camels also allow their body temperature to rise by several degrees during the day, instead of sweating to stay cool, and then lose the extra heat during the cool night. This saves a great deal of water. Finally, there are physiological adaptations related to water. Many desert animals produce very concentrated urine and dry droppings, minimizing water loss. Some, like the kangaroo rat of North America, obtain almost all the water they need from the seeds they eat. Understanding these strategies is important for conservation, particularly as climate change brings more frequent heatwaves, and it is even inspiring engineers who design cooling systems for buildings."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M25-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"How does reverse osmosis remove salt from seawater?","o":["by boiling the water and condensing the steam","by pushing water through membranes that block salt","by adding chemicals that absorb salt","by freezing the seawater"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Described in the lecture.","h":"أغشية تحجز الملح."},{"n":89,"s":"What challenge does the lecturer mention about solar-powered desalination?","o":["Solar panels do not work in hot weather.","Solar energy is too expensive in Saudi Arabia.","Solar energy is not available at night.","Solar panels damage the membranes."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'the sun does not shine at night'.","h":"انعدام الشمس ليلًا."},{"n":90,"s":"Why is brine an environmental concern?","o":["It contains too much fresh water.","It can harm marine life if released carelessly.","It produces greenhouse gases.","It blocks shipping routes."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"يضر بالحياة البحرية."}],"t":"Lecture: the rise of renewable desalination","sc":["Situation: An engineering lecturer speaks to students. (290 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. In countries with very limited fresh water, desalination, the removal of salt from seawater, has become essential. Saudi Arabia is one of the world's largest producers of desalinated water, which supplies most of the drinking water in many of its cities. However, desalination has traditionally depended on large amounts of fossil fuel energy, so researchers are now focusing on making it cleaner. There are two main desalination technologies. The older one, thermal desalination, works by heating seawater until it evaporates and then condensing the steam as fresh water. It is reliable but uses a great deal of energy. The newer technology, reverse osmosis, pushes seawater at high pressure through special membranes that allow water molecules to pass but block salt. Reverse osmosis typically uses much less energy than thermal methods, and it now accounts for most new desalination capacity worldwide. The next step is to power desalination with renewable energy. Solar energy is an obvious choice in sunny regions, and several plants now combine solar panels with reverse osmosis. A major challenge, however, is that the sun does not shine at night, while desalination plants work most efficiently when they operate continuously. Possible solutions include storing energy in batteries, combining solar with wind power, or connecting plants to a wider electricity grid with a high share of renewables. Another environmental issue is brine, the very salty water left over after desalination. If brine is released carelessly into the sea, it can harm marine life near the outlet. Engineers are developing ways to dilute it more effectively and even to extract useful minerals from it. In short, the future of desalination lies not only in producing more water, but in producing it with less energy and less environmental impact."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Therefore","For example","However","In addition"],"x":["Many students find mathematics difficult at first. ______, with regular practice, most of them become more confident."],"k":2,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second idea contrasts with the first.","h":"However للتضاد."},{"n":92,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) After that, the bread is shaped and left to rise again.","(2) Making traditional bread begins with mixing flour, water, salt and yeast.","(3) The dough is then kneaded and left to rise for an hour.","(4) Finally, it is baked in a hot oven until golden."],"k":2,"ty":"best order","e":"begins → then → After that → Finally.","h":"ترتيب الخطوات."},{"n":93,"s":"The (A) information in these leaflets (B) is (C) very (D) usefully.","o":["information","is","very","usefully"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"An adjective is needed after 'is': useful.","h":"الصفة useful."},{"n":94,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["In addition","However","Instead","Otherwise"],"x":["The new metro line will reduce traffic in the city centre. ______, it will lower air pollution."],"k":0,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence adds another benefit.","h":"In addition للإضافة."},{"n":95,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) Because of this, many visitors now come to see the restored buildings.","(2) Twenty years ago, the old district was in poor condition.","(3) The government then launched a project to restore its historic houses.","(4) Local craftsmen used traditional materials such as coral stone and wood."],"k":3,"ty":"best order","e":"past → project → method → result.","h":"الماضي ثم المشروع ثم النتيجة."},{"n":96,"s":"The manager (A) asked us (B) to (C) finishing the work (D) quickly.","o":["asked","to","finishing","quickly"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"ask someone to + base verb: finish.","h":"to + المصدر."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Public libraries offer many services to their communities. (2) They provide free access to books and computers. (3) They organize reading programmes for children. (4) Some writers become famous at a young age. (5) Many libraries also host workshops and lectures."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Famous young writers do not develop the point about library services.","h":"الكتّاب المشهورون خارج الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Many people own cars.","Walking is one of the easiest ways to stay healthy.","Heart disease has many causes.","Some shoes are expensive."],"x":["______ It can be done at any age, requires no special equipment and can easily be fitted into a daily routine. Even a thirty-minute walk each day can improve heart health."],"k":1,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe the benefits of walking.","h":"المشي سهل ومفيد."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["This experience has strengthened my wish to study medicine.","Hospitals are open twenty-four hours.","Some patients stay for weeks.","Nurses wear uniforms."],"x":["Volunteering at a hospital taught me a great deal. I learned how to communicate with patients who were anxious or in pain, and I saw how doctors and nurses work together as a team. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It draws a personal conclusion.","h":"خلاصة شخصية."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some offices have large windows.","In fact, research shows that tired workers make more mistakes and work more slowly.","Many people work in teams.","Productivity is measured in different ways. Teacher Section — Mock 25"],"x":["Many people believe that working longer hours always leads to greater productivity. ______ Taking regular breaks and getting enough rest can therefore make people more effective."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"Contrasting evidence before 'therefore'.","h":"دليل مخالف قبل therefore."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
